```

We detach candidate by replacing it with lost_child.

In Rust, I set `bubble_start` before detaching:
```rust
bubble_start = cand.borrow().parent_node.as_ref().unwrap().upgrade().unwrap();
candidate = cand;
```

Then detach:
```rust
let is_left = bubble_start.borrow().left_child_node.as_ref()
    .map_or(false, |l| Rc::ptr_eq(l, &candidate));
if is_left {
    bubble_start.borrow_mut().left_child_node = lost_child.clone();
} else {
    bubble_start.borrow_mut().right_child_node = lost_child.clone();
}
```

This is correct.

After detaching and placing candidate at node's position, candidate's parent_node is updated to node's old parent. So `bubble_start` is still the original parent (somewhere in the tree), and `candidate` is now at node's old position.

The inner loop walks from `bubble_start` up to `candidate`. Since `bubble_start` was below `candidate` in the original tree (it was in node's subtree), and candidate is now at node's position, `bubble_start` is still below `candidate`. So walking up from `bubble_start` will reach `candidate`.

